  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.55,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["J9", "Q2"],
      "pins": ["J9.2", "Q2.3"],
      "nets": ["VCC_OUT", "GND"],
      "observed": "The net VCC_OUT (the fan supply pin, J9 pin 2) appears only once on the whole schematic and is not connected to the high‑side switch Q2. The PMOS Q2 has its source at VCC (pin 2) and its gate at Q_PMOS_GSD (pin 1, driven by Q3/R1), but its remaining terminal (pin 3, the drain that should carry the switched fan voltage) is wired to a GND symbol instead of to VCC_OUT.",
      "expected": "In a high‑side PMOS fan‑power switch (spec: 'ATtiny‑driven fan controller'), the drain of Q2 must connect to VCC_OUT so that, when the MCU pulls Q_PMOS_GSD low via Q3, VCC is switched to the fan's VCC_OUT pin (J9 pin 2). The drain must not be tied to GND.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "J9 'FAN' pin 2 = VCC_OUT (the label VCC_OUT occurs nowhere else in the drawing)"},
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Q2 (PMOS): pin 2 = VCC, pin 1 = Q_PMOS_GSD, pin 3 tied to the GND symbol above it"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "an ATtiny‑driven fan controller"}
      ],
      "impact": "The fan's power pin (J9 pin 2) is left floating, so the fan never receives supply and cannot run. Worse, when the MCU enables the switch (Q_PMOS_GSD pulled low), Q2 turns on and shorts the VCC rail directly to GND through the PMOS, causing over‑current / regulator dropout rather than driving the fan.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing supports one anomaly: the fan supply net VCC_OUT (J9 pin 2) is not connected to the high‑side PMOS switch Q2, whose drain instead appears tied to GND, leaving the fan unpowered and creating a VCC‑to‑GND short whenever the switch is enabled."
}

